#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192335 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cebc16-84c3-5ec8-b1f1-5f9c51a69d94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "89d0136d-63d1-540b-a3be-acff2d0bbbb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-11027" :question/problem "For the function $g(x,y,z) = 2 (x + 1)e^z+ yz$, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial z}$ at $(1,1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z$, treating all other variables as constants.\n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂g}{∂z} &= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(2(x + 1)e^{z} + yz) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(2(x + 1)e^{z}) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz) \\\\\n&= 2(x + 1)\\frac{∂}{∂z}(e^{z}) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz) \\\\\n&= 2(x + 1)e^{z} + y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂g}{∂z} \\mid _{(1, 1, 1)} &= 2(1 + 1)e^{1} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4e + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192336 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e960f6d1-5006-5ddc-ada2-59b46924bf73" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "0fd784a0-5146-5f7b-9936-8d3bc43d7062" :question/math-academy-id "e-11028" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ for $f(x,y) =x^2y\\ln{x}$." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x$, treating all other variables as constants.\n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}y\\ln x) \\\\\n&= y \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}\\ln x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}\\ln x) &= y \\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}) \\cdot \\ln x + x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln x)) \\\\\n&= y \\cdot ((2x) \\cdot \\ln x + x^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})) \\\\\n&= y(2x\\ln x + x) \\\\\n&= xy(2\\ln x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192341 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14cc6590-c2fd-5395-9481-0e5c0d9b8b8b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "fc8ba431-5a97-5f57-9a83-fdcf4ac98d39" :question/math-academy-id "e-11048" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y) =\\ln(y-x^2)$, evaluate $f_x(1,9)$." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x$, treating all other variables as constants.\n\nFirst, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(x, y) &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (y - x^{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{y - x^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y - x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{y - x^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y - x^{2}) &= \\frac{1}{y - x^{2}} \\cdot (y \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1) - \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{y - x^{2}} \\cdot (y \\cdot 0 - 2x) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2x}{y - x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,9)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(1, 9) &= -\\frac{2 \\cdot 1}{9 - 1^{2}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192345 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c6e3db-073b-5867-8137-1d8660b20887" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "6f7bdbcf-fb8e-5c70-b368-4e73cc94bdb2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11112" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{4x-y}{x+y^2}$, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(2,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x$, treating all other variables as constants.\n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4x - y}{x + y^{2}}) &= (\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x - y) \\cdot (x + y^{2}) - (4x - y) \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + y^{2}))/((x + y^{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4 \\cdot (x + y^{2}) - (4x - y) \\cdot 1)/((x + y^{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4x + 4y^{2} - 4x + y)/((x + y^{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4y^{2} + y)/((x + y^{2})^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\mid _{(2,-1)} &= (4(-1)^{2} + (-1))/((2 + (-1)^{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 1}{3^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e960f6d1-5006-5ddc-ada2-59b46924bf73" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5cbe4e7-0b60-5240-8c81-37ca3e825df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190994} {:answer/id #uuid "ecad333e-15c6-5795-811d-950b822f9ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190995} {:answer/id #uuid "f26c6cb2-0e72-596d-97ab-61be3ea29a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190996} {:answer/id #uuid "537c32f3-aaef-5c54-baf2-f06eabb90d42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190997} {:answer/id #uuid "667e4592-59e8-5fb2-aeef-533cd0c37555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5cbe4e7-0b60-5240-8c81-37ca3e825df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190994} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc79c67f-1245-5bb4-8883-fdf9baee5a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190993}] :question/id #uuid "d20c624a-1709-5495-88ea-45d0df97ea34" :question/math-academy-id "q-159751" :question/problem "For the function $f(x, y) = ye^{xy} - x^{2}y^{2}$, evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1, 1)$."}